          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain the process of thermoregulation that occurs when the body temperature rises above normal.',
          answer:
            'When body temperature rises above normal, thermoreceptors detect this and signal the hypothalamus, which triggers heat-loss mechanisms: blood vessels near the skin surface dilate (vasodilation), increasing blood flow to the skin and heat loss by radiation; sweat glands increase sweat production, and evaporation of sweat from the skin cools the body; hair-erector muscles relax, lowering hairs to reduce the insulating layer of trapped air.',
          explanation: 'All of these responses increase the rate of heat loss from the body to the environment, working together to bring body temperature back down to its normal set point (around 37 °C) through negative feedback.',
