          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain the evidence for evolution provided by antibiotic resistance in bacteria, addressing how this observation directly demonstrates natural selection occurring in real time.',
          answer:
            'When a population of bacteria is exposed to an antibiotic, most bacteria are killed, but any bacteria that happen to already carry a rare, pre-existing mutation conferring resistance survive and reproduce, passing the resistance allele on to their offspring. Over successive generations of continued antibiotic exposure, the proportion of resistant bacteria in the population increases substantially — this can be directly observed happening over a relatively short period of time (days to months), rather than needing to be inferred indirectly from fossils or old data.',
          explanation: 'Because this entire process (variation, selection, and a resulting change in allele frequency) can be directly observed happening in real time in a laboratory or clinical setting, antibiotic resistance is considered one of the clearest and most directly observable pieces of evidence for natural selection actually occurring, rather than evidence that must be inferred from historical or indirect data.',
