          label: '4.11',
          topicId: 'life-sci-evolution',
          grade: 12,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain why natural selection cannot act directly on an organism’s genotype, but only on its phenotype.',
          answer:
            'Natural selection acts on the observable, functional characteristics (the phenotype) of an organism — such as its size, colouration, or behaviour — because it is the phenotype that directly interacts with the environment and determines whether an individual survives and reproduces successfully. The underlying genotype is not directly "visible" to the environment or to predators/competitors; it only has an effect on survival and reproduction indirectly, through the phenotype it produces.',
          explanation: 'This distinction matters because it means natural selection can only favour or disfavour a genotype indirectly, based on how well the phenotype it produces performs in a given environment — two different genotypes that happen to produce the same (or a very similar) phenotype would be equally favoured or disfavoured by selection, regardless of their underlying genetic differences.',
