          prompt: 'Using a Punnett square, explain why two parents who are both unaffected carriers of cystic fibrosis (Cc) have a 25% chance of having a child affected by cystic fibrosis with each pregnancy, showing your working.',
          answer:
            'Each Cc parent produces C and c gametes in equal (1/2) proportion. The Punnett square gives offspring genotypes CC, Cc, Cc, cc, each with 1/4 probability. Only the homozygous recessive genotype (cc) shows the cystic fibrosis phenotype, so the probability of an affected child is 1/4 = 25%.',
          explanation: 'The other three possible genotypes (CC and the two Cc combinations) all result in an unaffected (though sometimes carrier) phenotype, since C is dominant over c — this is why the probability of the specific homozygous recessive genotype (25%) directly gives the probability of the affected phenotype in this case.',
