          explanation: 'This is a further example of non-disjunction affecting the sex chromosomes, in this case resulting in a monosomy (only one sex chromosome present) rather than the trisomy seen in Klinefelter syndrome, illustrating that non-disjunction can produce either too many or too few copies of a chromosome, depending on which resulting gamete is involved in fertilisation.',
