          answer:
            'Because the genetic code is read in fixed groups of three bases (codons/triplets) starting from a fixed point, inserting or deleting a single base pair shifts the "reading frame" for every codon that follows it, changing essentially the entire sequence of codons (and hence amino acids) downstream of the mutation, often also introducing a premature stop codon partway through. A substitution mutation, in contrast, only changes a single codon (and therefore, at most, a single amino acid), while every codon before and after the substitution is read completely correctly, leaving the rest of the protein’s sequence unaffected.',
          explanation: 'This is why insertion and deletion mutations are generally described as far more disruptive than substitution mutations: a single substitution affects at most one amino acid in an otherwise normal protein, whereas an insertion or deletion typically corrupts the entire remaining sequence of the protein from that point onward.',
