          prompt: 'Using the long neck of the giraffe as an example, explain why Lamarck’s theory of "use and disuse" (inheritance of acquired characteristics) is now considered incorrect.',
          answer:
            'Lamarck proposed that individual giraffes stretched their necks during their lifetime to reach higher foliage, and that this acquired, stretched neck was then passed on directly to their offspring. This is now known to be incorrect because changes acquired by an individual during its own lifetime (such as a stretched neck from repeated reaching) do not alter that individual’s DNA in its reproductive (germ line) cells, and therefore cannot be biologically inherited by offspring — offspring inherit only the DNA sequence present in their parents’ gametes at the time of fertilisation, not any physical changes the parents underwent during their own lives.',
          explanation: 'Modern genetics confirms that heritable variation must already exist in the DNA passed on through gametes; Darwin’s theory (relying on pre-existing heritable variation and differential survival, rather than characteristics acquired and then inherited) correctly accounts for the evolution of the giraffe’s long neck without requiring this now-disproven mechanism.',
