          answer:
            'Flightless birds such as the ostrich possess small, reduced wings that are far too small and structurally weak to generate the lift needed for flight, yet they retain the same basic bone arrangement found in the wings of flying birds. This is best explained as an evolutionary remnant inherited from flying ancestors common to all birds, in which fully functional wings for flight were present; over evolutionary time, as ostrich ancestors adapted to a ground-dwelling lifestyle where flight was no longer needed (or advantageous), their wings were reduced, but a recognisable remnant of the ancestral wing structure remained.',
