          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain, in terms of auxin, why a plant stem laid horizontally will curve upward over time (negative geotropism).',
          answer:
            'When a stem is laid horizontally, gravity causes auxin to accumulate on the lower side of the stem. Shoot cells are stimulated (rather than inhibited) by higher concentrations of auxin, so the lower side of the stem elongates faster than the upper side, causing the stem to curve upward until it is once again growing vertically, against the direction of gravity.',
          explanation: 'This directional growth away from gravity (negative geotropism) helps ensure that a plant’s shoot system reliably grows upward toward light, regardless of the orientation in which the plant happens to have been knocked over or planted.',
