            '(1) Many animal-dispersed fruits are brightly coloured and produce a sweet, fleshy, edible pulp, attracting animals to eat the fruit; after passing through the animal’s digestive system undamaged, the enclosed seed(s) are deposited, often together with a supply of fertiliser in faeces, at a new location away from the parent plant. (2) Some seeds or fruits have hooks, spines or a sticky surface, allowing them to attach to the fur, feathers or skin of a passing animal, which then carries the seed away before it eventually falls off or is removed, dispersing it to a new location.',
          explanation: 'Both strategies rely on an animal’s own behaviour — eating, or simply brushing past — to disperse seeds over a potentially far greater distance than the plant itself could achieve by any passive means.',
