          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain the biological advantage of vegetative (asexual) propagation for a plant, and describe ONE natural example of this method of reproduction.',
          answer:
            'Vegetative propagation allows a plant to produce genetically identical offspring rapidly, without needing to rely on pollination, fertilisation, or the production and successful dispersal of seeds — all of which can fail or be delayed by unfavourable conditions, such as an absence of pollinators or drought. This allows a well-adapted plant to quickly colonise and dominate a favourable local area. Example: a strawberry plant produces long horizontal stems called runners (stolons), which grow along the ground and develop new roots and shoots at intervals, eventually becoming new, independent plants genetically identical to the parent.',
          explanation: 'Because vegetative propagation produces genetically identical offspring, it efficiently exploits an already stable, well-suited environment, but it introduces no new genetic variation into the population, unlike sexual reproduction, which is more advantageous for adapting to changing or unpredictable conditions.',
