          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain why many flowering plant species have evolved mechanisms that reduce self-pollination, even though self-pollination would guarantee fertilisation.',
          answer:
            'Repeated self-pollination over many generations tends to reduce genetic variation among a plant’s offspring, since it repeatedly combines gametes derived from a single parent’s own genetic material — gradually increasing the likelihood that harmful recessive alleles are expressed in the homozygous state (inbreeding depression), and reducing the population’s overall ability to adapt to changing conditions or new challenges, such as disease. Mechanisms such as anthers and stigmas maturing at different times within the same flower, self-incompatibility (where a plant’s own pollen fails to grow a pollen tube on its own stigma), or the physical separation of male and female flowers, all favour cross-pollination with a genetically different individual, producing more genetically varied offspring despite self-pollination being a more immediately "guaranteed" route to fertilisation.',
          explanation: 'This represents a trade-off between the reliability of self-pollination, which does not depend on an external pollinator or a nearby plant, and the long-term adaptive advantage of the greater genetic variation produced by cross-pollination — and most flowering plants have evolved toward favouring the latter.',
