          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Compare the type of skeleton found in an animal in the phylum Arthropoda with that of an animal in the phylum Chordata (for example, a vertebrate).',
          answer: 'Arthropods possess an exoskeleton — a hard external skeleton made of chitin, covering and supporting the body from the outside, which must be periodically shed (moulted) and replaced to allow for growth. Vertebrates (chordates) possess an endoskeleton — an internal skeleton, typically made of bone and/or cartilage, which grows gradually along with the rest of the body and does not need to be shed.',
          explanation: 'An endoskeleton generally allows for a greater potential maximum body size than an exoskeleton, since an exoskeleton would need to become impractically thick and heavy to support a very large animal, while an internal skeleton can scale up more efficiently as body mass increases.',
