          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Distinguish between the light-dependent and light-independent reactions of photosynthesis, referring to where in the chloroplast each occurs and whether each directly requires light.',
          answer: 'The light-dependent reactions occur on/in the thylakoid membranes and directly require light energy, which is absorbed by chlorophyll and used to split water and generate ATP and NADPH. The light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) occur in the stroma and do not directly require light; instead they use the ATP and NADPH supplied by the light-dependent reactions to fix carbon dioxide and produce glucose.',
