          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Explain the difference in composition between the fluid found in Bowman’s capsule (the filtrate) and the fluid found in the renal vein (blood leaving the kidney).',
          answer: 'The filtrate in Bowman’s capsule contains water, glucose, salts, urea and other small molecules, but no blood cells or large proteins (which are too large to be filtered out of the blood). The blood in the renal vein, by contrast, has had essentially all of its useful glucose and most of its water and salts reabsorbed back into it, retains its blood cells and large proteins throughout, and has a considerably lower concentration of urea than would otherwise be present, since urea has been selectively left behind in the filtrate to be excreted as urine.',
          explanation: 'Comparing these two fluids highlights the overall function of the nephron: it produces an initial filtrate containing both useful and waste substances, then selectively reabsorbs the useful ones back into the blood leaving via the renal vein, leaving mainly waste products and excess water to be excreted as urine.',
