          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Explain why the digestion of starch, which begins in the mouth, temporarily stops in the stomach.',
          answer: 'Salivary amylase, which digests starch in the mouth, only functions effectively at the near-neutral pH of saliva. Once food reaches the stomach, it becomes mixed with strongly acidic gastric juice, which denatures (inactivates) the salivary amylase, so no further starch digestion can occur until the food (now called chyme) reaches the small intestine, where it is neutralised and pancreatic amylase takes over.',
