          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'A person exercising at a steady, moderate pace can sustain this activity for a long period without their muscles tiring quickly, whereas a person sprinting at maximum effort tires within seconds. Explain this difference, referring to the type of respiration occurring in each case.',
          answer:
            'During steady, moderate exercise, the bloodstream can supply muscle cells with oxygen fast enough to meet their energy demand, so the muscles can respire aerobically and continue producing ATP efficiently for an extended period without a build-up of fatigue-causing waste products. During maximum-effort sprinting, the muscles’ energy demand rises far faster than the bloodstream can supply oxygen, forcing the muscles to rely heavily on anaerobic respiration, which produces ATP far less efficiently and causes lactic acid to accumulate rapidly, quickly leading to muscle fatigue.',
          explanation: 'This contrast shows why sustainable, aerobic exercise and short, maximal anaerobic effort place very different demands on the body, and why athletes train differently depending on whether their sport relies mainly on endurance (aerobic) or short bursts of maximum power (anaerobic).',
