          prompt: 'Describe THREE ways in which the branching structure of the bronchi and bronchioles contributes to efficient gaseous exchange in the lungs.',
          answer:
            '(1) Repeated branching into progressively narrower tubes distributes incoming air evenly to all regions of both lungs, rather than concentrating it in only a few areas. (2) This branching greatly increases the total number of tubes (and, ultimately, alveoli) that can be supplied with air within the limited space of the thoracic cavity, maximising the total surface area available for gaseous exchange. (3) The progressively narrowing diameter of the airways helps slow the flow of air by the time it reaches the alveoli, allowing more time for gaseous exchange to occur at the exchange surface.',
