          prompt: 'Explain how the excessive mucus production caused by chronic bronchitis (a long-term inflammation of the airways) can lead to a persistent cough and an increased risk of lung infection.',
          answer: 'Chronic inflammation of the bronchi causes the mucus-secreting cells lining the airway to produce far more mucus than normal, while the accompanying damage to the airway’s cilia reduces their ability to sweep this excess mucus up and out of the airway effectively. As a result, mucus accumulates within the airways, triggering a persistent cough as the body attempts to clear it, while the retained mucus also provides an environment in which inhaled bacteria can more easily multiply, increasing the risk of lung infection.',
