          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Distinguish between immigration and birth as two separate ways a population’s size can increase, and explain why this distinction matters when calculating a population’s net change over a given period.',
          answer:
            'Birth is the addition of new individuals to a population through reproduction occurring within that population, while immigration is the addition of individuals to a population through movement into it from elsewhere (individuals who were not born within that specific population). This distinction matters when calculating net population change because both factors must be counted separately alongside deaths and emigration; overlooking migration (immigration and emigration) and considering only births and deaths would give an inaccurate picture of the population’s true net change, particularly for populations in habitats that are not fully isolated, where migration can be a very significant contributor to overall population change.',
          explanation: 'This is why the standard formula for calculating net population change explicitly includes all four separate factors — births, deaths, immigration and emigration — rather than considering birth and death rates alone, since migration can substantially affect local population size independently of the population’s own reproductive and mortality rates.',
