          label: '4.6',
          topicId: 'life-sci-plant-tissues',
          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Describe the arrangement of vascular tissue (xylem and phloem) in a typical dicotyledonous root, compared with a typical dicotyledonous stem.',
          answer:
            'In a dicotyledonous root, the xylem forms a central, star- or cross-shaped core at the very centre of the root, with the phloem arranged in separate patches between the arms of the xylem — this central position helps the root resist the pulling (tension) forces exerted on it by the rest of the plant. In a dicotyledonous stem, the vascular tissue is instead arranged in a ring of separate vascular bundles nearer the outer edge of the stem, with the xylem positioned toward the inside of each bundle and the phloem toward the outside — this arrangement provides both flexibility and resistance to the bending forces a stem experiences.',
