          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Describe the pathway taken by water as it moves from the soil, through a plant, and out into the atmosphere.',
          answer: 'Water is absorbed from the soil by root hair cells, moves across the root cortex into the xylem at the centre of the root, and is then transported upward through the xylem of the root, stem and finally the veins of the leaf. From the leaf veins, water moves into the surrounding mesophyll cells, evaporates from their surfaces into the internal air spaces of the leaf, and diffuses out of the leaf into the atmosphere through open stomata.',
          explanation: 'This continuous pathway, from soil to atmosphere, is often referred to as the transpiration stream, since it is ultimately driven by the ongoing loss of water vapour from the leaf.',
