            'Root hairs greatly increase the surface area of the epidermis available for the absorption of water and dissolved mineral ions from the surrounding soil. Once absorbed, water and minerals move across the cortex, a wide region of loosely packed parenchyma cells, along a pathway toward the centrally located vascular tissue. The endodermis, a single layer of cells forming the innermost boundary of the cortex, has a waterproof band, the Casparian strip, running around the radial walls of each of its cells; this strip blocks water from moving further inward between the cells, forcing it instead to pass directly through the cell membranes and cytoplasm of the endodermal cells, allowing the plant to exert selective control over which dissolved substances are permitted to enter the xylem.',
          explanation: 'This arrangement means the endodermis functions as a final checkpoint, allowing the plant to filter out potentially harmful substances and regulate exactly which mineral ions are allowed to pass into the xylem for transport to the rest of the plant.',
