          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Describe the structure and function of skeletal muscle tissue.',
          answer: 'Skeletal muscle tissue is composed of long, cylindrical, striated (banded) muscle fibres, each containing many nuclei. Its contraction is voluntary (under conscious control); it is generally attached to bones by tendons, and its function is to contract and pull on these bones to bring about the movement of body parts.',
          explanation: 'The striped (striated) appearance of skeletal muscle comes from the highly organised, repeating arrangement of the protein filaments (actin and myosin) inside each muscle fibre, which slide past one another to produce contraction.',
