          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Describe the structure of a neuron, referring to its cell body and axon, and explain how its elongated shape suits it to carrying a nerve impulse rapidly over a long distance, such as from the spinal cord to a muscle in the foot.',
          answer:
            'A neuron has a cell body, containing the nucleus and most of the cell’s organelles, from which a single, often very long, thin extension called the axon projects. The axon carries a nerve impulse, as an electrical signal, away from the cell body toward the next neuron or an effector such as a muscle. Because the axon can be extremely long relative to the rest of the cell — in humans, a single axon can run the entire distance from the spinal cord down to a muscle in the foot — a nerve impulse generated at one end can be carried directly, as a single continuous signal along one cell, all the way to its distant destination, without needing to be relayed through a chain of many shorter cells.',
          explanation: 'Many axons are additionally wrapped in a fatty, insulating myelin sheath, which further increases the speed at which the nerve impulse can travel along such a long axon, allowing very rapid communication between distant parts of the body.',
