          answer: '(1) Over millions of years, the remains of dead organisms, buried and subjected to intense heat and pressure underground, can slowly be converted into fossil fuels, such as coal, oil and natural gas, storing that carbon underground for long periods. (2) Living trees can store large amounts of carbon within their wood for many decades, while marine organisms can incorporate carbon into calcium carbonate shells and skeletons, which may accumulate on the sea floor and eventually form limestone rock, storing that carbon for very long periods.',
