          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain what is meant by nitrogen fixation, and name TWO different natural mechanisms by which it occurs.',
          answer:
            'Nitrogen fixation is the process by which unreactive atmospheric nitrogen gas, which most plants cannot absorb or use directly, is converted into ammonium compounds that plants are able to absorb through their roots and use to build proteins and other nitrogen-containing molecules. Two natural mechanisms: (1) nitrogen-fixing bacteria, such as Rhizobium bacteria living within root nodules of leguminous plants, and free-living nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the soil, convert atmospheric nitrogen into ammonium compounds. (2) Lightning provides enough energy to combine atmospheric nitrogen with oxygen, forming nitrogen oxides that dissolve in rainwater and reach the soil in a usable form, though this mechanism contributes a much smaller share of total nitrogen fixation than bacteria do.',
          explanation: 'Because most plants cannot fix atmospheric nitrogen themselves, nitrogen fixation is the essential first step that makes the vast reservoir of atmospheric nitrogen gas available to enter the rest of the nitrogen cycle, and ultimately, food chains.',
