            'Osmosis is the diffusion of water molecules across a partially permeable membrane, from a region of higher water concentration (lower solute concentration) to a region of lower water concentration (higher solute concentration). Distilled water contains no dissolved solutes at all, so it has a much higher water concentration than the inside of the plant cell, and water will therefore move into the cell by osmosis. As water enters, the cell becomes turgid — its cytoplasm swells and presses outward against the rigid cell wall, which resists further expansion and prevents the cell from bursting, unlike what would happen to an animal cell under the same conditions.',
          explanation: 'This turgidity is important to plants, since the outward pressure of many turgid cells pressing against their cell walls helps provide structural support to non-woody parts of the plant, such as leaves and young stems, which would otherwise wilt.',
