          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Describe the structure and protective function of the epidermis (and its cuticle) covering the stem of a young plant.',
          answer: 'The stem epidermis is a single layer of tightly packed cells covering the outer surface of a young stem, most of which secrete a waxy, waterproof cuticle over their outer walls. This cuticle reduces uncontrolled loss of water from the stem surface, and together with the epidermis itself, forms a protective barrier against invading pathogens and mechanical damage; some epidermal cells may also bear small hairs (trichomes) that provide additional protection.',
          explanation: 'As a woody stem undergoes secondary growth, this epidermis is eventually replaced by tougher, corky bark tissue produced by the cork cambium, which continues to provide this same general protective function.',
