          topicId: 'life-sci-plant-tissues',
          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Describe the arrangement of vascular tissue in a typical monocotyledonous root, compared with a typical dicotyledonous root.',
          answer:
            'In a monocotyledonous root, the xylem and phloem are arranged in an alternating ring pattern (often with more than four points of xylem) around a large, central region of parenchyma tissue called the pith. In a dicotyledonous root, the xylem instead forms a solid, star- or cross-shaped core at the very centre of the root, with the phloem arranged in separate patches between the arms of the xylem, and there is no central pith.',
          explanation: 'This difference in vascular arrangement, together with differences in the arrangement of vascular tissue in their stems, is one of the key anatomical features used to distinguish monocotyledonous plants from dicotyledonous plants.',
