          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain, step by step, how the loss of water vapour from a leaf during transpiration ultimately results in water being drawn upward through the xylem from the roots.',
          answer:
            'As water vapour diffuses out of the leaf through the open stomata during transpiration, it is replaced by water evaporating from the moist surfaces of the mesophyll cells lining the internal air spaces of the leaf. As these mesophyll cells lose water, they draw in more water by osmosis from the xylem vessels running through the leaf veins, lowering the water pressure at the top of the xylem and creating a pulling force (tension). Because the water molecules within the xylem cohere strongly to one another, this tension is transmitted as a continuous pull down through the entire connected column of water in the xylem, all the way to the roots, drawing more water into the xylem from the root cells, which in turn draws more water into the roots from the surrounding soil by osmosis.',
          explanation: 'This entire sequence, from water vapour loss at the leaf to water uptake at the root, is driven ultimately by transpiration at the top of the plant, rather than by any pumping action occurring lower down.',
