          answer:
            'Outdoors, the greater light intensity causes the stomata to open wider, allowing more water vapour to escape; the higher temperature increases both the rate at which water evaporates from the mesophyll cells and the kinetic energy of the water vapour molecules, further increasing water loss; and the movement of air (wind) continuously carries away the layer of humid air that would otherwise build up around the stomata, maintaining a steep water vapour concentration gradient that keeps diffusion out of the leaf rapid. Indoors, the lower light intensity keeps the stomata more narrowly open, the cooler temperature slows evaporation, and the still, already humid air quickly reduces the concentration gradient once some water vapour has diffused out, all of which combine to keep the indoor plant’s transpiration rate much lower.',
