          answer: 'The waxy cuticle covering a leaf’s epidermis is a waterproof layer that reduces uncontrolled water loss (evaporation) directly through the epidermal cells, separately from water lost through the stomata. Because a xerophyte lives in an environment where water is scarce and must be conserved as much as possible, it typically develops a much thicker cuticle than a mesophyte, further reducing this route of water loss and helping the plant survive with a limited water supply. A mesophyte, having more reliable access to water, does not face the same pressure to minimise water loss to this extent, and so typically has a comparatively thinner cuticle.',
          explanation: 'This difference in cuticle thickness is one of several structural adaptations (alongside features such as sunken stomata and reduced leaf surface area) that distinguish xerophytes from plants adapted to less water-limited environments.',
