          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Describe the arrangement of tissues in a typical dicotyledonous leaf, in sequence from the upper epidermis to the lower epidermis.',
          answer:
            'The upper epidermis, usually covered by a waxy cuticle and lacking chloroplasts, lies at the top of the leaf. Beneath it lies the palisade mesophyll, made up of tightly packed, column-shaped, chloroplast-rich cells. Below the palisade mesophyll lies the spongy mesophyll, made up of loosely arranged cells with large interconnecting air spaces between them. At the base of the leaf lies the lower epidermis, which contains most of the leaf’s stomata, each flanked by a pair of guard cells. Vascular bundles (veins), containing xylem and phloem, run through the mesophyll between these layers.',
