          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Describe the functions of platelets and white blood cells in the blood.',
          answer: 'Platelets are small cell fragments involved in blood clotting; they help form a clot at the site of a wound to stop bleeding and to prevent pathogens from entering the body through the wound. White blood cells form part of the body’s immune system, defending against and destroying pathogens or other foreign material, for example, by engulfing them (phagocytosis) or by producing antibodies against them.',
