          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Explain why a food web gives a more realistic picture of the feeding relationships within an ecosystem than a single food chain does.',
          answer: 'A single food chain shows only one linear sequence of feeding relationships, as though each organism fed on, or was fed upon by, just one other species. In reality, most organisms feed on more than one type of food source and are themselves preyed upon by more than one predator. A food web links together many overlapping food chains within a community, showing these multiple, interconnected feeding relationships, and so represents the true complexity of feeding interactions within a real ecosystem far more accurately than a single food chain could.',
          explanation: 'This is also why the loss of a single species tends to affect an entire food web through several altered feeding relationships at once, rather than disrupting just one isolated chain of organisms.',
