          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain the difference between a pyramid of numbers and a pyramid of biomass, and describe ONE circumstance under which a pyramid of biomass could appear inverted.',
          answer:
            'A pyramid of numbers represents the number of individual organisms present at each trophic level, without regard to their size, whereas a pyramid of biomass represents the total dry mass of living material present at each trophic level. A pyramid of biomass can appear inverted in an ecosystem where the producers have a very small standing biomass at any one moment but reproduce and are consumed extremely rapidly — for example, phytoplankton in the open ocean, whose tiny total biomass at any single snapshot in time can be less than the biomass of the zooplankton consuming them, even though the phytoplankton actually produce far more total biomass over an entire year.',
          explanation: 'This shows that even a pyramid of biomass, unlike a pyramid of energy, can occasionally give a misleading, inverted picture if it is measured at only a single point in time rather than accumulated over a longer period.',
