          answer: 'Cellulose is a polysaccharide made of long, unbranched chains of glucose monomers joined by condensation reactions (glycosidic bonds); many of these parallel chains are held tightly side by side by numerous hydrogen bonds, bundling them into strong, rope-like microfibrils. This bundled, cross-linked structure gives cellulose great tensile strength, making it well suited to forming the rigid cell wall that supports and protects plant cells.',
