          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain why DNA must be replicated during interphase before a cell can undergo mitosis, and explain what would happen if a cell attempted to divide without first replicating its DNA.',
          answer:
            'DNA must be replicated during interphase so that each of the two daughter cells produced by mitosis can receive a complete, identical copy of the parent cell’s full genetic information. Without this replication, there would only be enough DNA for a single complete set of chromosomes; if such a cell nonetheless attempted to divide, its genetic material would have to be split between the two daughter cells, so each daughter cell would receive only an incomplete set of chromosomes and genetic information, very likely leaving it unable to carry out its normal functions, since it would be missing genes needed to produce essential proteins.',
          explanation: 'This is exactly why DNA replication, occurring during the S-phase of interphase, is an essential preparatory step that must always precede a successful mitotic division.',
