          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain how the rate of mitotic cell division in different tissues of the human body, comparing skin cells with mature nerve cells, relates to how frequently each tissue needs to be replaced or repaired.',
          answer:
            'Tissues that are frequently damaged, worn away or naturally shed — such as the skin, which is constantly exposed to friction and damage — contain cells that divide by mitosis very frequently, continuously replacing lost or damaged cells to maintain the tissue’s structure and function. Mature nerve cells, by contrast, divide extremely rarely, or in most cases not at all once fully differentiated, since they are not typically lost through everyday wear in the same way, and their complex, highly specialised structure and connections make them difficult to precisely replace; this is also why damage to nerve tissue is generally so difficult for the body to repair through further cell division.',
          explanation: 'This connects mitosis’s role in repair and replacement directly to the differing regenerative capacities of different tissue types, an application of the general growth-and-repair significance of mitosis.',
