          answer: '(1) Xylem vessel elements are dead at maturity, whereas phloem sieve tube elements are living cells. (2) Xylem vessels have thick, lignified cell walls, whereas phloem sieve tube elements have thinner, unlignified walls and are supported instead by adjacent companion cells.',
          explanation: 'These structural differences reflect their different transport roles: xylem’s dead, lignified vessels suit the purely passive transport of water, while phloem’s living sieve tubes and companion cells suit the active loading and unloading of dissolved sugars.',
