          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Describe the internal structure of a dicotyledonous root in cross-section, referring to the epidermis, the cortex, the endodermis and the vascular tissue.',
          answer:
            'The outermost layer is the epidermis, including its root hairs, which absorbs water and mineral ions from the soil. Beneath this lies the cortex, a wide region of parenchyma cells used mainly for the storage of starch and other substances, through which absorbed water and minerals pass on their way toward the centre of the root. The endodermis is a single layer of cells surrounding the central vascular tissue, which regulates and directs the movement of water and dissolved minerals into the xylem. At the very centre lies the vascular tissue itself, with the xylem forming a central, star-shaped core and the phloem arranged in separate patches between its arms.',
