          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Compare the venation (arrangement of veins) typically found in a monocotyledonous leaf with that of a dicotyledonous leaf, and state ONE other feature, other than venation, that can be used to distinguish monocotyledonous from dicotyledonous plants.',
          answer:
            'A monocotyledonous leaf typically has parallel venation, in which the main veins run alongside one another, parallel to the length of the leaf, as seen in grasses and maize. A dicotyledonous leaf typically has net (reticulate) venation, in which the veins branch repeatedly and interconnect, forming a network pattern across the leaf, as seen in bean or sunflower leaves. Other distinguishing feature: the number of cotyledons (seed leaves) present in the seed or seedling — monocotyledons have one cotyledon, while dicotyledons have two (the arrangement of vascular bundles in the stem — a ring in dicots, scattered in monocots — is also acceptable).',
          explanation: 'Leaf venation is one of the most visually obvious ways to quickly distinguish monocotyledonous from dicotyledonous plants in the field, in addition to less immediately visible features such as root structure or floral parts.',
