          answer: 'Companion cells occur in phloem tissue. A mature sieve tube element loses its nucleus and most of its organelles, so it cannot control its own metabolism or make the ATP and proteins it needs; the companion cell alongside it has dense cytoplasm, a nucleus and many mitochondria and is joined to the sieve tube by plasmodesmata, so it supplies the energy and controls the loading and unloading of sugars for the sieve tube element.',
          explanation: 'The sieve tube is a hollow conducting channel while the companion cell is its life-support and control unit, so the two work together as a single physiological unit in translocation.',
