          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A farmer accidentally removes a complete ring of bark, including all the phloem, from around the trunk of a young peach tree. The xylem deeper inside the trunk is undamaged.',
          prompt: 'Predict what will happen above and below the ring over the following months, and explain each prediction.',
          answer: 'Above the ring the tree stays alive for some time and the bark may swell just above the cut, because the xylem is intact and still carries water and mineral salts up to the leaves while the sugars made in photosynthesis move down only as far as the break and accumulate there. Below the ring the roots begin to starve because translocation of sucrose through the phloem to the roots has been cut off, so the roots use up their stored food, stop growing and eventually die, and once the roots fail the whole tree dies from lack of water and mineral salts.',
          explanation: 'Ringing separates the two transport pathways, because upward transport of water in dead xylem is unaffected while downward transport of organic food in living phloem is destroyed, so the damage shows first in the organs below the cut.',
