          marks: 5,
          context: 'Parenchyma occurs in the mesophyll of a leaf, in the cortex of a stem and in the flesh of a potato tuber.',
          prompt: 'Explain how the same basic tissue can carry out photosynthesis in the leaf but storage in the potato tuber, referring to the features the cells share and to the ways in which they differ.',
          answer: 'All parenchyma cells share the same basic plan, since they are living, thin-walled, roughly spherical cells with a large central vacuole and air spaces between them, and they stay unspecialised enough to take on different functions. In the leaf the parenchyma cells contain many chloroplasts, are arranged as a palisade layer close to the upper surface and are surrounded by intercellular air spaces that allow carbon dioxide to reach them, so they photosynthesise. In the potato tuber the cells are underground and receive no light, so they contain leucoplasts instead of chloroplasts and are packed with starch grains formed from sucrose delivered by the phloem, so they act as a storage tissue.',
          explanation: 'Parenchyma is the least specialised plant tissue, so its function in a particular organ depends chiefly on which plastids are present and on where the tissue lies in the plant.',
