          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Distinguish between a tendon and a ligament in terms of the structures they connect and the type of fibre that predominates in each.',
          answer: 'A tendon attaches a muscle to a bone and consists mainly of closely packed, inelastic white collagen fibres, so that the full force of the contracting muscle is transmitted to the bone without stretching. A ligament joins bone to bone at a joint and contains a high proportion of yellow elastic fibres, so it can stretch slightly to allow movement while still holding the bones together and preventing dislocation.',
          explanation: 'The difference in fibre type follows directly from function, because transmitting force requires inelastic tissue while a joint that must move requires limited elasticity.',
