          marks: 5,
          context: 'A Weddell seal hunting under the ice of Antarctic waters has a layer of blubber up to 8 cm thick beneath its skin.',
          prompt: 'Name the type of connective tissue that makes up this layer, and explain FOUR ways in which the tissue benefits the seal.',
          answer: 'The layer is adipose tissue. It insulates the seal by trapping heat and reducing heat loss to the freezing water; it stores fat as a long-term energy reserve for periods when the seal does not feed; it cushions and protects the underlying organs against knocks and pressure; and because fat is less dense than water it increases buoyancy, so the seal uses less energy to stay afloat between dives.',
