          answer: 'The first, longer sound is caused by the closing of the atrioventricular valves, namely the bicuspid and tricuspid valves, as the ventricles begin to contract, and the second, sharper sound is caused by the closing of the semilunar valves at the openings of the aorta and pulmonary artery as the ventricles relax. Blood cannot flow back into the left atrium because the rising pressure in the contracting left ventricle forces the flaps of the bicuspid valve shut, and the tendinous cords attached to the papillary muscles hold the flaps so that they cannot be turned inside out into the atrium, so the only exit left for the blood is the aorta.',
          explanation: 'The heart sounds are produced by valves snapping shut rather than by muscle contraction, and the one-way flow of blood through the heart depends entirely on pressure differences acting on those valves.',
