          prompt: 'Explain why the pulse rate increased during the run, and explain why it did not return to the resting value immediately after she stopped.',
          answer: 'During the run the leg muscles respired much faster and needed more oxygen and glucose while producing more carbon dioxide; the higher carbon dioxide concentration in the blood was detected and the heart was stimulated to beat faster and more strongly, so more blood was pumped to the muscles each minute to deliver oxygen and glucose and to remove carbon dioxide and heat. The rate did not drop back at once because the muscles had respired anaerobically in part and had built up lactic acid and an oxygen debt, so the heart had to keep beating rapidly to carry extra oxygen to the muscles to break down the lactic acid and to remove the carbon dioxide still dissolved in the blood, and only once these returned to normal, after about ten minutes, did the pulse rate return to 74 beats per minute.',
          explanation: 'Heart rate tracks the metabolic demand of the tissues rather than the movement itself, which is why recovery takes several minutes and why the recovery time is shorter in a fitter person.',
