          answer: 'Primary consumers receive 10% of 40 000 = 4 000 kJ·m⁻² per year, and secondary consumers receive 10% of 4 000 = 400 kJ·m⁻² per year. Energy that is not passed on is lost as heat released during respiration, and it is lost in undigested material passed out as faeces as well as in urine and in the parts of the organism not eaten, while some is also lost to decomposers when organisms die.',
          explanation: 'The 10% rule is applied once for each feeding step, so the calculation must pass through the primary consumer level, and the losses explain why only about a tenth of the energy can be built into new tissue at the next level.',
