          answer: 'At every transfer from one trophic level to the next roughly 90% of the energy is lost, mainly as heat given off in respiration but also in faeces, urine and uneaten parts, so only about 10% is built into new body tissue. After four or five transfers so little of the original energy trapped by the grass remains that it could not support a viable population of an additional predator, since each animal would have to find more food than the level below could supply. Energy flow is one-way because the energy enters as sunlight, passes along the chain and is finally lost to the surroundings as heat, which cannot be trapped again by the producers, so the ecosystem needs a continuous supply of sunlight. Matter cycles because the carbon, nitrogen and other atoms in the bodies of organisms are released by decomposers when wastes and dead remains break down and are then absorbed and reused by producers, so the same atoms are used over and over again.',
          explanation: 'The contrast between one-way energy flow and cyclical matter flow is the central organising idea of ecosystem ecology and explains both the shortness of food chains and the necessity of decomposers.',
