          marks: 7,
          context: 'In a wetland food web, reeds are eaten by water snails and by reed-eating insects; the snails are eaten by fish and by ibises; the insects are eaten by frogs and by ibises; the frogs are eaten by herons; the fish are eaten by herons. A disease then kills almost all the frogs.',
          prompt: 'Predict and fully explain the effect of the loss of the frogs on the insects, on the ibises and on the herons, and use your answer to explain what is meant by the stability of an ecosystem.',
          answer: 'The number of reed-eating insects would rise sharply at first because one of their two predators has been removed, so fewer are eaten and more survive to breed, which would in turn increase the grazing pressure on the reeds. The ibises would probably increase, because they can feed on the now more plentiful insects as well as on snails, so their food supply improves. The herons would decrease, at least at first, because they have lost one of their two food sources and must depend entirely on the fish, so competition among the herons for fish increases and some starve or move away; heavier predation on the fish may then reduce the fish population as well. This shows that a stable ecosystem is one that can absorb such a change and return to a balanced state, and stability increases with the number of species and the number of alternative feeding pathways, because a consumer with several food sources, like the ibis, is far less affected by the loss of one prey species than a specialist would be.',
          explanation: 'Predicting knock-on effects requires tracing each arrow in both directions from the species removed, and the comparison between the ibis and the heron shows why biodiversity is the basis of ecosystem stability.',
