          marks: 7,
          context: 'Energy passed on per square metre per year in two ecosystems. Estuary: producers 25 000 kJ, primary consumers 3 000 kJ, secondary consumers 240 kJ. Pine plantation: producers 25 000 kJ, primary consumers 1 250 kJ, secondary consumers 90 kJ.',
          prompt: 'Calculate the percentage of the producers’ energy passed to the primary consumers in each ecosystem, compare the efficiency of the two ecosystems, and suggest TWO reasons for the difference.',
          answer: 'Estuary: 3 000 ÷ 25 000 × 100 = 12%. Pine plantation: 1 250 ÷ 25 000 × 100 = 5%. The estuary is therefore more than twice as efficient at transferring energy from the producer level to the primary consumers, and it also passes on more energy at the next step, 240 kJ against 90 kJ. One reason is that the producers in the estuary are mainly soft algae and young reed tissue that are easily eaten and easily digested, whereas much of the biomass of a pine plantation is wood and bark containing lignin and cellulose that few animals can digest, so a large part of the plant material is never eaten by herbivores at all and passes straight to the decomposers. A second reason is that the pine plantation is a monoculture of one species with few herbivores adapted to feed on it, so a smaller proportion of the plant tissue produced each year is consumed, while the estuary supports a far greater diversity of grazers that harvest a much larger share of the available production.',
          explanation: 'Transfer efficiency depends on how digestible and how accessible the producer tissue is to consumers, which is why aquatic systems with soft-bodied producers typically transfer a larger percentage than forests dominated by woody tissue.',
