          answer: 'In the lock-and-key model each enzyme has an active site with a specific three-dimensional shape, and only a substrate whose shape fits exactly into that active site can bind to it, rather as only one key fits a particular lock; once the enzyme-substrate complex forms, the reaction takes place and the products leave, freeing the unchanged enzyme to be used again. Sucrase cannot digest starch because the active site of sucrase is shaped to fit the sucrose molecule only; the much larger starch molecule has a different shape, cannot fit into the active site and therefore cannot be broken down by this enzyme.',
