          answer: 'The two glucose molecules are joined by condensation: a hydroxyl group from one glucose and a hydrogen atom from the other are removed, one molecule of water is released and the two units become linked to form the disaccharide maltose. The bond formed is a glycosidic bond. The opposite reaction, in which a water molecule is added to split maltose back into two glucose molecules, is called hydrolysis and is catalysed by the enzyme maltase during digestion.',
