          prompt: 'Explain why it is important that mitosis produces daughter cells with the same chromosome number as the parent cell, and state what would happen to the daughter cells if a pair of chromatids failed to separate during anaphase.',
          answer: 'The chromosome number must stay the same because the chromosomes carry the coded instructions for all the proteins of the organism, so every new body cell needs a complete set in order to function correctly and to grow, and the chromosome number characteristic of the species must be maintained in all body cells from one generation of cells to the next. If a pair of chromatids failed to separate, both chromatids would move to the same pole, so one daughter cell would receive an extra chromosome and the other would be missing one; both cells would then have an abnormal chromosome number, the instructions they carry would be incorrect and the cells would function abnormally or die.',
