          context: 'Onion root tips were grown in a solution of colchicine, a chemical that prevents spindle fibres from forming. The roots stopped growing longer, and many cells were seen with chromosomes scattered in the cytoplasm.',
          prompt: 'Explain, phase by phase, why the cells could not complete mitosis, and explain why the root stopped growing in length.',
          answer: 'Prophase could still take place, since the chromosomes coiled, became visible and the nuclear membrane broke down, but without spindle fibres the chromosomes could not be attached to the poles and so could not be arranged along the equator, and metaphase could not be completed. Anaphase could not take place at all, because the chromatids are separated and drawn to the poles by the shortening of the spindle fibres, so the chromatids remained joined and the chromosomes simply lay scattered in the cytoplasm. Since the chromosomes were never separated into two groups, no two nuclei could form in telophase and the cytoplasm did not divide, so no new cells were produced. The root stopped growing longer because growth in length depends on the continual production of new cells by mitosis in the apical meristem followed by their elongation, and once mitosis was blocked no new cells were added.',
          explanation: 'Tracing the effect of a blocked structure through each phase shows that the spindle is the mechanical link between the chromosomes and the poles, without which every later stage fails.',
