          prompt: 'Calculate the percentage of the cell cycle spent in mitosis, and explain why the cell must spend so much longer in interphase than in mitosis.',
          answer: 'Percentage spent in mitosis = 2 ÷ 24 × 100 = 8,3%. The cell spends far longer in interphase because a great deal of work must be completed before division is possible: the cell must grow to full size and make new cytoplasm, it must manufacture proteins and enzymes and increase the number of organelles such as mitochondria and ribosomes so that each daughter cell will receive enough of them, it must replicate the whole of its DNA exactly during the S phase so that each chromosome consists of two identical chromatids, and it must store the ATP that will be needed to drive the movements of mitosis. Mitosis itself is only the mechanical separation of material that has already been prepared and duplicated, so it takes comparatively little time.',
          explanation: 'Interphase is an active preparation stage rather than a resting stage, and the ratio of the two times reflects how much more work is involved in building a cell than in dividing one.',
