          context: 'In a transverse section of a dicotyledonous leaf the layers from top to bottom are: a single layer of flat cells covered by a thick waxy layer; a layer of tall, closely packed green cells; a layer of loosely arranged green cells with large air spaces; and a lower layer of flat cells containing many small openings, each flanked by two bean-shaped green cells.',
          prompt: 'Name the tissues that make up each of these four layers, and explain THREE structural features of the leaf shown in the description that make it efficient at photosynthesis.',
          answer: 'The top layer is the upper epidermis covered by a cuticle; the tall green cells are palisade mesophyll; the loosely arranged green cells are spongy mesophyll; and the lower layer is the lower epidermis containing stomata, each flanked by two guard cells. The leaf is efficient at photosynthesis because the upper epidermis is transparent and only one cell thick, so light passes straight through it to reach the palisade layer; because the palisade cells are packed closely together directly beneath that surface and are full of chloroplasts, so as much light as possible is absorbed; and because the air spaces of the spongy mesophyll are continuous with the stomata in the lower epidermis, so carbon dioxide can diffuse quickly to every photosynthesising cell while the leaf is also broad and thin, giving a large surface area for light absorption and short diffusion distances.',
          explanation: 'The layered structure of a leaf is a sequence of adaptations addressing the three requirements of photosynthesis in turn, namely light, carbon dioxide and water delivered by the veins.',
