          answer: 'The mopane tree is supported mechanically by dead, lignified tissue. Its trunk contains a large amount of secondary xylem, or wood, in which the vessel and fibre walls are heavily thickened with lignin, together with sclerenchyma fibres, and this rigid framework is permanent and can bear the mass of the crown and resist the bending force of the wind. The sunflower seedling has very little lignified tissue and is supported mainly hydraulically, by the turgor of its living parenchyma cells assisted by the flexible collenchyma below the epidermis of the young stem, each turgid cell pressing outwards against its cell wall so that the tissue as a whole becomes firm. When the soil dries out the seedling loses more water by transpiration than it absorbs, its cells lose water by osmosis and become flaccid, the outward pressure on the cell walls is lost and the stem can no longer hold itself up, so the plant wilts. The mopane tree does not collapse because the strength of its lignified walls does not depend on the water content of the cells at all, since the supporting cells are already dead.',
