          answer: 'A double circulation means that the blood passes through the heart twice for every complete circuit of the body. In the pulmonary circuit the right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood to the lungs and it returns to the left atrium, and in the systemic circuit the left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood to the rest of the body and it returns to the right atrium. The advantage is that blood which has passed through the narrow capillaries of the lungs, where it has lost much of its pressure, is returned to the heart and pumped again, so it can be sent to the body organs at high pressure and therefore flows quickly. In a fish the blood passes through the gill capillaries and then travels on to the body without being repressurised, so it moves slowly, whereas the mammal delivers oxygen and glucose rapidly enough to support the high metabolic rate needed to maintain a constant body temperature.',
