          answer: 'The fish excrete nitrogenous waste as ammonia, and their uneaten food and faeces are broken down by decomposers to release more ammonia. In a new tank there is no established population of nitrifying bacteria on the filter and gravel, so the ammonia is not converted and its concentration builds up rapidly; ammonia is highly toxic because it damages the gills and interferes with the excretion of waste by the fish, so they suffocate and die. During the weeks of cycling a population of nitrifying bacteria grows in the filter: one group oxidises the ammonia to nitrites, which are also toxic, and a second group then oxidises the nitrites to nitrates, which are far less harmful and can be kept low by partial water changes or taken up by water plants. Once both groups are numerous enough to convert the ammonia as fast as it is produced, the tank is safe for fish.',
